 ### [Mesh BVH](1062-mesh-bvh.md)
-A GPU-accelerated BVH for triangle meshes, serving as a geometric backend for SDFs, enabling fast distance queries for high-frequency surface details.
+Mesh BVH is a GPU-accelerated bounding volume hierarchy designed for triangle meshes to perform efficient distance queries, serving as the geometric backend for signed distance field (SDF) representations in volume rendering pipelines. It enables fast evaluation of point-to-mesh distance, closest point location, and topological properties (sign, winding number) required for ray-marching or grid-based rendering. Unlike hierarchical voxel traversals that operate on implicit grid structures, this method operates directly on explicit triangle geometry, making it suitable for high-frequency surface details that would be prohibitive to store in a dense or even sparse voxel grid.
 
 ### [Hierarchical Digital Differential Analyzer (HDDA)](1122-hierarchical-digital-differential-analyzer-hdda.md)
-A specialized traversal for Sparse Voxel Octrees (OpenVDB) that uses multi-resolution leapfrogging and DDA at each node level to skip non-intersected nodes.
+The Hierarchical Digital Differential Analyzer (HDDA) is a specialized ray-traversal algorithm for sparse voxel grids (specifically OpenVDB) that performs multi-resolution leapfrogging to efficiently determine voxels or tiles intersected by a ray. It leverages the compile-time fixed tree configuration of VDB to inline and collapse the hierarchical traversal, reducing the overhead of vertical tree navigation. Unlike generic BVH or KD-tree traversals, HDDA is tightly coupled to the B+tree structure of VDB, using DDA at each node level (InternalNode2, InternalNode1, Leaf) to skip non-intersected nodes and return leaf intersections in front-to-back order.
 
